Q: How to allow users to embed an iframe directly in your WordPress theme&#39;s pages? A: <p>For those coming in to this question, the more robust way of including an iframe in WordPress is to only allow a user to define the iframe's attributes, rather than giving them an open book. Wanton use of iframes will open an entire can of worms of security problems if one is not careful.</p>
<p>By defining only the allowed attributes, one can then filter those using some manner of allow-list, especially for the <code>src</code>. For example, one could constrain the iframe only to <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>.</p>
<p>That aside, if one absolutely must, normally-disallowed HTML can be allowed by adjusting the <code>wp_kses_allowed_html</code> filter. <strong>However</strong>, this is <em>extremely dangerous</em>.</p>
<p>Therefore, please ensure that one properly contextualizes the <code>wp_kses</code> output, and only allow iframes in that single instance.</p>
<p>In <code>functions.php</code>:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php

/**
 * Allow iframe tags in HTML under controlled conditions.
 */
add_filter('wp_kses_allowed_html', function($tags, $context) {
  if ( $context === 'allow_iframe' ) {
    $tags['iframe'] = [
      // 'align'           =&gt; true,
      // 'allow'     =&gt; true,
      // 'allowfullscreen' =&gt; true,
      'class'     =&gt; true,
      // 'frameborder'     =&gt; true,
      'height'    =&gt; true,
      'id'        =&gt; true,
      // 'marginheight'    =&gt; true,
      // 'marginwidth'     =&gt; true,
      'name'      =&gt; true,
      'scrolling' =&gt; true,
      'src'       =&gt; true,
      'style'     =&gt; true,
      'width'     =&gt; true,
      'title'     =&gt; true,
    ];
  }
  return $tags;
}, 10, 2);
</code></pre>
<p>and <em>at the point of rendering the iframe</em>:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?= wp_kses($iframe_content, wp_kses_allowed_html( $context = 'allow_iframe' ))?&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Produce JSON by RESTful web service in Spring Boot? A: <p>Could you please check you have the following dependency in your <code>pom.xml</code> ?</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;com.fasterxml.jackson.dataformat&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;jackson-dataformat-xml&lt;/artifactId&gt;
    &lt;version&gt;2.6.3&lt;/version&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Also you can have more information about how Spring boot handles Java object to JSON on spring boot website : <a href="https://spring.io/guides/gs/rest-service/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://spring.io/guides/gs/rest-service/</a></p>
<blockquote>
<p>The Greeting object must be converted to JSON. Thanks to Spring’s HTTP
message converter support, you don’t need to do this conversion
manually. Because <a href="http://wiki.fasterxml.com/JacksonHome" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Jackson 2</a> is on the classpath, Spring’s
<code>MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter</code> is automatically chosen to convert
the Greeting instance to JSON.</p>
</blockquote>
